   Hmm, there's a subtlety. In Perelman's paper, the L-length uses the integrand √τ (R + |γ'|²) with τ the time parameter along the curve, and γ parametrized by τ ∈ [0, τ̄]. In flat Euclidean space, L(γ) = ∫_0^τ̄ √τ |γ'|² dτ. The minimizer with γ(0)=0, γ(τ̄)=x: by Cauchy-Schwarz, ∫ √τ |γ'|² dτ ≥ (∫ √τ γ' dτ · ... hmm. Actually the minimizing curve: Euler-Lagrange gives d/dτ(2√τ γ') = 0 → √τ γ' = const = c → γ' = c/√τ → γ(σ) = 2c√σ. With γ(τ̄) = 2c√τ̄ = x, c = x/(2√τ̄). So γ(σ) = (√σ/√τ̄) x. That's the D7 Gaussian path! And L = ∫ √τ |c|²/τ dτ = |c|² · 2√τ̄ = (|x|²/(4τ̄)) · 2√τ̄ = |x|²/(2√τ̄). Yes. And l = L/(2√τ̄) = |x|²/(4τ̄). 

