   From the D7 proof gaussian_length_le, the equality case: P.length ≥ |q|²/(2√τ), with equality iff ∫ of the completing-square integrand = 0 iff √σ|γ'-c/√σ|² = 0 a.e. iff γ' = c/√σ a.e. iff (√σ·γ)' = c... wait γ' = c/√σ where c = q/(2√τ), so γ' = q/(2√τ √σ) — exactly gaussianVelocity. Then γ = 2√σ · q/(2√τ) = (√σ/√τ) q, by FTC (γ(0)=0). So a.e. equality of derivative + γ(0)=0 + continuity gives γ(σ) = gaussianPath everywhere. This is provable: if P is a minimizer, then P.length = gaussianLPath.length (since minimizer + gaussian path is admissible... wait IsLMinimizer says P.length ≤ Q.length for all Q, and gaussian path has length |q|²/(2√τ) ≤ P.length by gaussian_length_le. So P.length = |q|²/(2√τ) = gaussian path length). Then the completing-square integrand integrates to zero and is nonnegative → zero a.e. → derivative condition a.e. → γ(σ) = gaussianPath q τ σ by FTC.

   This is a genuinely nice "uniqueness / straight ray" theorem. But it's some work. The FTC part: we know γ' = gaussianVelocity a.e. on (0,τ), both continuous... To conclude γ = gaussianPath on [0,τ]: both have γ(0) = 0 and a.e. equal derivatives, and both are absolutely continuous? The LPath has ContinuousOn + HasDerivAt on (0,τ) + IntervalIntegrable velocity. mathlib has `intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt_of_le` — for functions f with HasDerivAt f f' on Ioo and IntervalIntegrable f'. So γ(σ) = ∫_0^σ γ' = ∫_0^σ gaussianVelocity = gaussianPath σ. That works via integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt_of_le at each σ.

