   So the reduced volume Ṽ(τ) = ∫ τ^{-n/2} e^{-ℓ(x,τ)} dx = (4π)^{n/2} ∫ K = (4π)^{n/2}. That's constant but equals (4π)^{n/2}, not 1. Perelman's reduced volume: Ṽ(τ) = ∫ τ^{-n/2} e^{-ℓ(q,τ)} dq. In flat ℝⁿ: Ṽ(τ) = τ^{-n/2} ∫ e^{-|x|²/4τ} dx = τ^{-n/2} (4πτ)^{n/2} = (4π)^{n/2}. Constant = (4π)^{n/2}, independent of τ. The task says "hence the reduced volume is constant = 1 for all τ (full computation with the D10 Gaussian toolbox)". Hmm — so the task wants the normalized version = 1. I can define the **normalized reduced volume** V(τ) = (4πτ)^{-n/2} ∫ e^{-ℓ} dx (i.e., with the Gaussian normalization) and prove it equals 1 identically (this is exactly `gaussianKernel_integral` from D10). And also prove the Perelman reduced volume ∫ τ^{-n/2} e^{-ℓ} = (4π)^{n/2}, constant in τ. Both are good; the "= 1" claim corresponds to the normalized one, which coincides with the D10 mass-1 theorem. I'll present both: `reducedVolume_unscaled` constant = (4π)^{n/2}, and `reducedVolume` (normalized) = 1 for all τ > 0. The task literally says "the reduced volume integrand τ^{-n/2} exp(-ℓ) coincides with the Gaussian — hence the reduced volume is constant = 1 for all τ". So the integrand τ^{-n/2} e^{-ℓ} coincides with the Gaussian e^{-|x|²/4τ} (un-normalized Gaussian). Hmm "coincides with the Gaussian" — τ^{-n/2} exp(-ℓ) = τ^{-n/2} exp(-|x|²/4τ), which is the Gaussian function exp(-|x|²/4τ) times the τ^{-n/2} prefactor; and this is exactly (4π)^{n/2} · gaussianKernel. So "coincides with the Gaussian [heat kernel]" up to the constant (4π)^{n/2}. Then "hence the reduced volume is constant = 1 for all τ" — with the D10 normalization. I'll write it as: integrand = (4π)^{n/2} · gaussianKernel n τ x, hence ∫ integrand = (4π)^{n/2} (constant), and normalized reduced volume = ∫ gaussianKernel = 1.

